> >> Privateers were lawful pirates that were endorsed by the crown.
> 
> > In theory. In practice, rarely. Most of them quickly degenerated
> > into simple looters.

Those who had a written permit were effectively naval mercenaries for
their "sponsoring" nation who received safe haven and non-persecution
as recompensation for risking their necks weakening the "enemy" and
tithing part of their spoils. Only worked as long as both sides stuck
to the agreement and it seems to me that neither side was very
scrupulous about kicking their "partner" where it hurt as long as they
thought they could get away with it.

I suspect that "Sir" Francis Drake was viewed mainly as a rich thug
with low morals (for a thug) _by his compatriots_. I don't think that
the spanish sea-farers and the carribean inhabitants who had their
ships taken and their settlements plundered by him had such a high
opinion of him.

The privateers were typically self-serving and tended not to play to
the rules unless they absolutely had to (a rope 'round the neck can be
a strong persuasive argument); making them less "lawful" than a
Mafioso, who in NetHack's alignment system would probably still be
labelled as a chaotic Rogue.

> Getting written permission from some higher authority before starting
> to pillage, murder, and loot is lawful behaviour.  Lawful evil
> alright, but still lawful.

AFAIK, several of the famous privateers had been notorious pirates
_before_ they were taken into royal service.

Privateering is of course a time-honoured practice: the most popular
olden-times privateers in these here parts are Klaus Störtebeker and
his Likedeelers. "Des lieben Gottes Freund und aller Welt Feind"
(God's friend and all the world's foe) only became his motto after his
privateering job was over and he returned to free-lance pirating,
which ended up with him losing his head on command of those who had
earlier protected him as "their" privateer.

